<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Adolf I, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1649">1649</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Adolf I was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a> from 1680 to 1697, a member of the Albertine branch of the Wettin dynasty. He succeeded his father Augustus, the founder of the line, and completed the ducal residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neu-augustusburg">Neu-Augustusburg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>. His court was known for music and opera, a tradition continued by his successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-adolf-i-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels.jpg" alt="Johann Adolf I, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels" width="241" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Adolf_I._von_Sachsen-Wei%C3%9Fenfels_(a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Adolf was born in Halle on 2 November 1649, the first son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels">Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, and his first wife, Anna Maria of Mecklenburg-Schwerin. On 25 October 1671 he married Johanna Magdalena of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> at Altenburg. They had eleven children, several of whom died in infancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His father died on 4 June 1680, and the family lost the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> secularised. Johann Adolf first turned to the unfinished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neu-augustusburg">Schloss Neu-Augustusburg</a>, where building resumed on 18 August 1680; the chapel was consecrated on 1 November 1682 and the castle was finally paved in 1694. A large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> staged opera in German from 1685, and beside the castle he laid out what became the most important formal garden in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> of his time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> grew into an economic as well as an administrative centre.</p><p>Following his father's example, he kept Johann Philipp Krieger as court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a>. He noticed the musical talent of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">Georg Friedrich Händel</a>, son of his court surgeon, and urged the father to let the boy train in music.</p><p>His inheritance had been reduced: his father left Barby to a younger son, Heinrich, and the old duke's debts forced Johann Adolf in 1687 to sell Burg bei <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. When Elector Johann Georg III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> refused to recognise the collateral lines of his cousins, Johann Adolf secured his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> and his seat in the Upper Saxon council through the Contract of Torgau of 12 May 1681 and two later agreements signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his wife's death in 1686 he married Christiane Wilhelmine of Bünau at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> on 3 February 1692, a morganatic marriage made by contract; at his request she was created an Imperial Countess in 1697. They had no children. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> on 24 May 1697, and his three surviving sons, Johann Georg, Christian and Johann Adolf II, ruled the duchy in turn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Adolf I of Saxe-Weissenfels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weißenfels</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1680" aria-label="Halle (Saale) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Adolf I, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,11.97&amp;year=1680">What was here in 1680? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
